"JEE Advanced's 2010 IIT-JEE 2010 Paper 1 Offline Paper's All Questions with solutions provider ExamSIDE.Com","paperTitle":"IIT-JEE 2010 Paper 1 Offline","nextq":"jXcbXHYOkXNoAopq","questions":[{"country":"in","examGroup":"jee","question_id":"pOSrjOhNahZ2cmEC","exam":"jee-advanced","marks":3,"negMarks":0,"subjectGroup":null,"subject":"chemistry","chapterGroup":"organic-chemistry","languages":["en"],"chapter":"basics-of-organic-chemistry","year":2010,"paperTitle":"IIT-JEE 2010 Paper 1 Offline","difficulty":"medium","topicName":null,"type":"integer","examDate":null,"question":{"en":{"content":"The total number of cyclic isomers possible for a hydrocarbon with the molecular formula C<sub>4</sub>H<sub>6</sub> is","options":[],"correct_options":[],"answer":"5","explanation":null}},"permalink":"the-total-number-of-cyclic-isomers-possible-for-a-hydrocarbo-2010-marks-3-posrjohnahz2cmec.htm","timeAllotted":200000,"paperId":"iit-jee-2010-paper-1-offline","topic":"isomerism","isOutOfSyllabus":false,"isBonus":false,"yearKey":"iit-jee-2010-paper-1-offline","active":true},{"country":"in","examGroup":"jee","question_id":"6VW9qVDND6TSBxaZ","exam":"jee-advanced","marks":4,"negMarks":0,"subjectGroup":null,"subject":"chemistry","chapterGroup":"inorganic-chemistry","languages":["en"],"chapter":"chemical-bonding-and-molecular-structure","year":2010,"paperTitle":"IIT-JEE 2010 Paper 1 Offline","difficulty":"medium","topicName":null,"type":"integer","examDate":null,"question":{"en":{"content":"Based on VSEPR theory, the number of 90 degree F-Br-F angles in BrF<sub>5</sub> is","options":[],"correct_options":[],"answer":"0","explanation":"<p>According to VSEPR, <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.375ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"4.954ex\" height=\"1.92ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -683 2189.6 848.6\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"msub\"><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"42\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-42\"></use><use data-c=\"72\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-72\" transform=\"translate(708,0)\"></use><use data-c=\"46\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-46\" transform=\"translate(1100,0)\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(1786,-150) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"35\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-35\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> has square pyramidal structure with axial plane containing a lone pair and fluorine. The other four fluorine are arranged in square planner configuration around central metal atom. Thus, <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.375ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"4.954ex\" height=\"1.92ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -683 2189.6 848.6\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"msub\"><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"42\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-42\"></use><use data-c=\"72\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-72\" transform=\"translate(708,0)\"></use><use data-c=\"46\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-46\" transform=\"translate(1100,0)\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(1786,-150) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"35\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-35\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> assumes square pyramidal shape where the valence electron pairs surrounding an atom tend to repel each other and will, therefore, adopt an arrangement that minimises this repulsion, thus, determining the molecule's geometry. All four planar bonds <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.566ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"12.735ex\" height=\"2.262ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -750 5628.9 1000\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\"><use data-c=\"28\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-28\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\" transform=\"translate(389,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"46\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-46\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(1264.2,0)\"><use data-c=\"2212\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2212\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\" transform=\"translate(2264.4,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"42\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-42\"></use><use data-c=\"72\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-72\" transform=\"translate(708,0)\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(3586.7,0)\"><use data-c=\"2212\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2212\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\" transform=\"translate(4586.9,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"46\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-46\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(5239.9,0)\"><use data-c=\"29\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-29\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> will reduce from <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.05ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"3.25ex\" height=\"1.649ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -707 1436.6 729\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"msup\"><g data-mml-node=\"mn\"><use data-c=\"39\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-39\"></use><use data-c=\"30\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-30\" transform=\"translate(500,0)\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" transform=\"translate(1033,393.1) scale(0.707)\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\"><use data-c=\"2218\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2218\"></use></g></g></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> to <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.05ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"5.01ex\" height=\"1.674ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -718 2214.6 740\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"msup\"><g data-mml-node=\"mn\"><use data-c=\"38\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-38\"></use><use data-c=\"34\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-34\" transform=\"translate(500,0)\"></use><use data-c=\"2E\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2E\" transform=\"translate(1000,0)\"></use><use data-c=\"38\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-38\" transform=\"translate(1278,0)\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" transform=\"translate(1811,404.1) scale(0.707)\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\"><use data-c=\"2218\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2218\"></use></g></g></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> after lone pair - bond pair repulsion.</p>\n\n<p>So, there are no 90 -degree <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.186ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"10.975ex\" height=\"1.731ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -683 4850.9 765\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"46\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-46\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(875.2,0)\"><use data-c=\"2212\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2212\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\" transform=\"translate(1875.4,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"42\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-42\"></use><use data-c=\"72\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-72\" transform=\"translate(708,0)\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(3197.7,0)\"><use data-c=\"2212\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2212\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\" transform=\"translate(4197.9,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"46\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-46\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> angles in <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.375ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"4.954ex\" height=\"1.92ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -683 2189.6 848.6\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"msub\"><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"42\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-42\"></use><use data-c=\"72\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-72\" transform=\"translate(708,0)\"></use><use data-c=\"46\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-46\" transform=\"translate(1100,0)\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(1786,-150) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"35\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-35\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container>.</p>\n<br><img src=\"https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/1000/image/6y3zli1lw5lm5uf/369c10de-1365-477a-aeb8-739c818e5cf5/fd5b9b60-1180-11ef-a5ed-a35ade190016/file-6y3zli1lw5lm5ug.png?format=png\" data-orsrc=\"https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/image/6y3zli1lw5lm5uf/369c10de-1365-477a-aeb8-739c818e5cf5/fd5b9b60-1180-11ef-a5ed-a35ade190016/file-6y3zli1lw5lm5ug.png\" loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;display:block;margin:0 auto;max-height:40vh;vertical-align:baseline\" alt=\"IIT-JEE 2010 Paper 1 Offline Chemistry - Chemical Bonding &amp; Molecular Structure Question 25 English Explanation\" srcset=\"https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/500/image/6y3zli1lw5lm5uf/369c10de-1365-477a-aeb8-739c818e5cf5/fd5b9b60-1180-11ef-a5ed-a35ade190016/file-6y3zli1lw5lm5ug.png?format=png 500w, https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/750/image/6y3zli1lw5lm5uf/369c10de-1365-477a-aeb8-739c818e5cf5/fd5b9b60-1180-11ef-a5ed-a35ade190016/file-6y3zli1lw5lm5ug.png?format=png 750w, https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/1000/image/6y3zli1lw5lm5uf/369c10de-1365-477a-aeb8-739c818e5cf5/fd5b9b60-1180-11ef-a5ed-a35ade190016/file-6y3zli1lw5lm5ug.png?format=png 1000w, https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/1500/image/6y3zli1lw5lm5uf/369c10de-1365-477a-aeb8-739c818e5cf5/fd5b9b60-1180-11ef-a5ed-a35ade190016/file-6y3zli1lw5lm5ug.png?format=png 1500w, https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/2000/image/6y3zli1lw5lm5uf/369c10de-1365-477a-aeb8-739c818e5cf5/fd5b9b60-1180-11ef-a5ed-a35ade190016/file-6y3zli1lw5lm5ug.png?format=png 2000w, https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/2500/image/6y3zli1lw5lm5uf/369c10de-1365-477a-aeb8-739c818e5cf5/fd5b9b60-1180-11ef-a5ed-a35ade190016/file-6y3zli1lw5lm5ug.png?format=png 2500w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 767px) 100vw, 720px\" decoding=\"async\">"}},"permalink":"based-on-vsepr-theory-the-number-of-90-degree-f-br-f-angles-2010-marks-4-6vw9qvdnd6tsbxaz.htm","timeAllotted":200000,"paperId":"iit-jee-2010-paper-1-offline","topic":"hybridization-and-vsepr-theory","isOutOfSyllabus":false,"isBonus":false,"yearKey":"iit-jee-2010-paper-1-offline","active":true},{"country":"in","examGroup":"jee","question_id":"pjk6z5DCOvXt9CBU","exam":"jee-advanced","marks":3,"negMarks":0.75,"subjectGroup":null,"subject":"chemistry","chapterGroup":"physical-chemistry","languages":["en"],"chapter":"thermodynamics","year":2010,"paperTitle":"IIT-JEE 2010 Paper 1 Offline","difficulty":"easy","topicName":null,"type":"mcq","examDate":null,"question":{"en":{"content":"The species which by definition has <b>ZERO</b> standard molar enthalpy of formation at 298 K is","options":[{"identifier":"A","content":"Br<sub>2</sub> (g)"},{"identifier":"B","content":"Cl<sub>2</sub> (g)"},{"identifier":"C","content":"H<sub>2</sub>O (g)"},{"identifier":"D","content":"CH<sub>4</sub> (g)"}],"correct_options":["B"],"answer":null,"explanation":"<p>The standard molar enthalpy of formation, denoted as <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.954ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"5.001ex\" height=\"2.574ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -716 2210.4 1137.6\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"394\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-394\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"msubsup\" transform=\"translate(833,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D43B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D43B\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(973.9,363) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"2218\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2218\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(864,-276.6) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"1D453\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D453\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container>, is defined as the change in enthalpy when one mole of a compound is formed from its constituent elements in their standard states under standard conditions (298 K and 1 atm pressure). By definition, the standard molar enthalpy of formation of a pure element in its most stable form at 298 K is zero.</p>\n\n<p>Looking at the options:</p>\n\n<ul>\n\n<li><strong>Br<sub>2</sub> (g)</strong> - Bromine's standard state at room temperature is liquid (Br<sub>2</sub>(l)), not gas.</li>\n\n<li><strong>Cl<sub>2</sub> (g)</strong> - Chlorine's standard state at room temperature is a gas, which means the standard molar enthalpy of formation for Cl<sub>2</sub>(g) is indeed zero.</li>\n\n<li><strong>H<sub>2</sub>O (g)</strong> - Water in gaseous form is not a basic element but a compound of hydrogen and oxygen, so its enthalpy of formation is not zero.</li>\n\n<li><strong>CH<sub>4</sub> (g)</strong> - Methane, represented here, is also a compound composed of carbon and hydrogen, thus its enthalpy of formation is also not zero.</li>\n\n</ul>\n\n<p>Therefore, the correct option is <strong>Option B</strong>, Cl<sub>2</sub> (g), as chlorine gas is a diatomic molecule and an element in its standard state at 298 K, and as such, its standard molar enthalpy of formation is zero.</p>"}},"permalink":"the-species-which-by-definition-has-zero-standard-molar-enth-2010-marks-3-pjk6z5dcovxt9cbu.htm","timeAllotted":200000,"paperId":"iit-jee-2010-paper-1-offline","topic":"fundamentals-of-thermodynamics","isOutOfSyllabus":false,"isBonus":false,"yearKey":"iit-jee-2010-paper-1-offline","active":true},{"country":"in","examGroup":"jee","question_id":"mGkPK8eEtDZeWCI0","exam":"jee-advanced","marks":3,"negMarks":0.75,"subjectGroup":null,"subject":"chemistry","chapterGroup":"physical-chemistry","languages":["en"],"chapter":"thermodynamics","year":2010,"paperTitle":"IIT-JEE 2010 Paper 1 Offline","difficulty":"easy","topicName":null,"type":"mcqm","examDate":null,"question":{"en":{"content":"Among the following, the intensive property is (properties are)","options":[{"identifier":"A","content":"molar conductivity"},{"identifier":"B","content":"electromotive force"},{"identifier":"C","content":"resistance"},{"identifier":"D","content":"heat capacity"}],"correct_options":["A","B"],"answer":null,"explanation":"<p>An intensive property is a property that is independent of the amount of mass or size of the system. In contrast, an extensive property is dependent on the size or amount of mass in the system. Let's analyze each option based on this definition:</p>\n\n<ul>\n\n<li><strong>Molar Conductivity</strong>: Molar conductivity, denoted as <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.357ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"3.163ex\" height=\"1.977ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -716 1397.8 873.8\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"msub\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"39B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-39B\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(727,-150) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"1D45A\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D45A\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container>, is a measure of the ionic conductivity of an electrolyte solution divided by the concentration of the electrolyte. Since it is normalized by the amount of substance, it does not depend on the total amount of the substance present in the solution. Thus, molar conductivity is an intensive property.</li>\n\n<li><strong>Electromotive Force (EMF)</strong>: EMF is the voltage generated by a battery or by the magnetic force according to Faraday’s Law of electromagnetic induction. It refers to a potential difference and does not depend on the quantity of material or size of the battery. Therefore, EMF is considered an intensive property.</li>\n\n<li><strong>Resistance</strong>: Resistance, denoted by <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.048ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.717ex\" height=\"1.593ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -683 759 704\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D445\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D445\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container>, measures how much a material opposes the flow of electric current. It depends on the material’s length, cross-sectional area, and resistivity. For instance, a longer wire has greater resistance. Therefore, resistance is an extensive property.</li>\n\n<li><strong>Heat Capacity</strong>: Heat capacity is the amount of heat required to change the temperature of a substance by a certain temperature interval. It is dependent on the amount of substance present, and therefore, it is an extensive property. The specific heat capacity, however, is intensive since it is the heat capacity per unit mass.</li>\n\n</ul>\n\n<p>From the options provided:\n\n<ul>\n\n<li>Option A (Molar Conductivity) is intensive.</li>\n\n<li>Option B (Electromotive Force) is intensive.</li>\n\n<li>Option C (Resistance) is extensive.</li>\n\n<li>Option D (Heat Capacity) is extensive.</li>\n\n</ul>\n\n<p>Therefore, the intensive properties among the given options are Option A (Molar Conductivity) and Option B (Electromotive Force).</p></p>"}},"permalink":"among-the-following-the-intensive-property-is-properties-a-2010-marks-3-mgkpk8eetdzewci0.htm","timeAllotted":200000,"paperId":"iit-jee-2010-paper-1-offline","topic":"fundamentals-of-thermodynamics","isOutOfSyllabus":false,"isBonus":false,"yearKey":"iit-jee-2010-paper-1-offline","active":true}],"ids":["pOSrjOhNahZ2cmEC","6VW9qVDND6TSBxaZ","pjk6z5DCOvXt9CBU","mGkPK8eEtDZeWCI0"],"title":"IIT-JEE 2010 Paper 1 Offline | JEE Advanced Year Wise Previous Years Questions","canonical":"https://app/past-years/jee/the-total-number-of-cyclic-isomers-possible-for-a-hydrocarbo-2010-marks-3-posrjohnahz2cmec.htm"}